#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE FOURTH STAGE

Now when they had eaten and drunk, and had chatted a little longer, their guide said to them, The day is wearing on; if you think it best, let us get ready to be going. So they got up to go, and the little boys went ahead; but Christiana forgot to take her bottle of spirits with her, so she sent her little boy back to get it. Then Mercy said, I think this is a place for losing things: here Christian lost his roll, and here Christiana left her bottle behind her. Sir, what is the cause of this? Their guide answered, The cause is sleep, or forgetfulness: some sleep when they should keep awake, and some forget when they should remember; and this is the very reason why, at the resting-places, some pilgrims often come off losers in some things. Pilgrims should watch, and remember what they have already received, in the midst of their greatest enjoyments; but because they fail to do so, their rejoicing often ends in tears, and their sunshine in a cloud: witness the story of Christian at this place.

When they had come to the place where Mistrust and Timorous met Christian, to persuade him to go back for fear of the lions, they saw what looked like a stage, and in front of it, towards the road, a broad plaque with some verses written on it, and underneath, the reason why that stage had been put up in that place. The verses were these:

#quote[“Let him that sees this stage, take heed \
Unto his heart and tongue; \
Lest, if he do not, here he speed \
As some have long agone.”]

The words underneath the verses were, “This stage was built as a place to punish those who, through timidity or mistrust, are afraid to go further on pilgrimage. Also, on this stage both Mistrust and Timorous were burned through the tongue with a hot iron, for trying to hinder Christian on his journey.”

Then Mercy said, This is very like the saying of the Beloved: “What will He do to you, and what will be added to you, O deceitful tongue? Sharp arrows will come from the warrior, with burning coals of the broom tree!” Ps 120:3, 4.

So they went on till they came within sight of the lions. Now Mr. Great-Heart was a strong man, so he was not afraid of a lion; but when they had come up to the place where the lions were, the boys, who had been going in front, were now glad to shrink behind, for they were afraid of the lions; so they stepped back and went behind. At this their guide smiled and said, What is this, my boys? Do you love to go in front when no danger is near, and love to come behind as soon as the lions appear?

Now, as they went on, Mr. Great-heart drew his sword, meaning to make a way for the pilgrims in spite of the lions. Then someone appeared who, it seems, had taken it on himself to back the lions; and he said to the pilgrims’ guide, What has brought you here? Now the name of that man was Grim, or Bloody-man, because he killed pilgrims; and he was of the race of the giants.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then the pilgrims’ guide said, These women and children are going on pilgrimage, and this is the way they must go; and go it they shall, in spite of you and the lions.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Grim]: This is not their way, and they shall not go on it. I have come out to stand against them, and to that end I will back the lions.]

Now, to tell the truth, because of the fierceness of the lions, and the grim bearing of the man who backed them, this way had lately been little used, and was almost grown over with grass.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana said, Though the highways have been deserted until now, and though travelers have in times past been made to walk through by-paths, it must not be so now that I have arisen, now that I have arisen a mother in Israel. Judg 5:6, 7.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Grim]: Then he swore by the lions that it would be so; and so he told them to turn aside, for they would not be allowed to pass there.]

But Great-Heart their guide went at Grim first, and laid on him so heavily with his sword that he forced him to retreat.

#speech[#smallcaps[Grim]: Then the one who was trying to back the lions said, Will you kill me on my own ground?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It is the King’s highway that we are on, and it is on this road that you have placed the lions; but these women and these children, though weak, shall keep on their way in spite of your lions. And with that he gave him another downward blow, and brought him to his knees. With this blow he also broke the giant’s helmet, and with the next he cut off an arm. Then the giant roared so hideously that his voice frightened the women, and yet they were glad to see him lie sprawling on the ground. Now the lions were chained, and so could do nothing by themselves. So when old Grim, who had meant to back them, was dead, Mr. Great-Heart said to the pilgrims, Come now, and follow me, and no harm shall come to you from the lions. So they went on, but the women trembled as they passed by them; the boys also looked as if they would die; but they all got by without further harm.]

Now, when they came within sight of the Porter’s lodge, they soon reached it; but they hurried all the more after this to get there, because it is dangerous to travel there at night. So when they had come to the gate, the guide knocked, and the Porter called out, Who is there? But as soon as the guide had said, It is I, the Porter knew his voice and came down, for the guide had often come there before as a conductor of pilgrims. When he had come down, he opened the gate; and seeing the guide standing just in front of it (for he did not see the women, because they were behind him), he said to him, Why, Mr. Great-Heart, what is your business here so late at night? I have brought some pilgrims here, he said, where by my Lord’s command they must lodge. I would have been here some time ago, if I had not been opposed by the giant who used to back the lions. But after a long and tedious fight with him, I have cut him down, and have brought the pilgrims here in safety.

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: Won’t you come in, and stay till morning?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: No, I will go back to my Lord tonight.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Oh, sir, I cannot bring myself to be willing for you to leave us in our pilgrimage: you have been so faithful and loving to us, you have fought so bravely for us, you have been so hearty in counseling us, that I shall never forget your kindness to us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy said, Oh, if only we could have your company to our journey’s end! How can poor women like us hold out on a road as full of troubles as this one is, without a friend and defender?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: Then James, the youngest of the boys, said, Please, sir, let us persuade you to go with us and help us, because we are so weak, and the way is so dangerous.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I am at my Lord’s command; if he assigns me to be your guide all the way, I will gladly attend you. But here you failed at first; for when he told me to come this far with you, you should then have begged him to let me go all the way with you, and he would have granted your request. However, for now I must leave you; and so, good Christiana, Mercy, and my fine children, farewell.]

Then the Porter, Mr. Watchful, asked Christiana about her country and her family. And she said, I came from the city of Destruction. I am a widow, and my husband is dead; his name was Christian, the pilgrim. What! said the Porter, was he your husband? Yes, she said, and these are his children; and this, pointing to Mercy, is one of my townswomen. Then the Porter rang his bell, as he usually does at such times, and one of the young women, whose name was Humble-Mind, came to the door; and the Porter said to her, Go and tell them inside that Christiana, the wife of Christian, and her children have come here on pilgrimage. So she went in and told them. But oh, what a noise of gladness there was inside when the young woman had barely let that drop from her lips!

So they hurried to the Porter, for Christiana was still standing at the door. Then some of the gravest of them said to her, Come in, Christiana, come in, you wife of that good man; come in, you blessed woman, come in, with all who are with you. So she went in, and those who were her children and companions followed her. When they had gone in, they were taken into a large room, where they were asked to sit down; so they sat down, and the heads of the house were called to see and welcome the guests. Then they came in, and when they understood who the guests were, they greeted each other with a kiss, and said, Welcome, you#super[+] vessels of the grace of God; welcome to us, your#super[+] friends.

Now, because it was rather late, and because the pilgrims were weary from their journey, and also faint from the sight of the fight and of the terrible lions, they wanted to get ready for bed as soon as they could. No, said those of the household, first refresh yourselves with a morsel of food; for they had prepared a lamb for them, with the customary sauce that goes with it, Exod 12:21; John 1:29; for the Porter had heard of their coming beforehand, and had told those inside. So when they had eaten supper, and ended their prayer with a psalm, they asked if they might go to bed.

But let us, said Christiana, if we may be so bold as to choose, be in the room that was my husband’s when he was here; so they took them up there, and they all slept in one room. When they had lain down, Christiana and Mercy talked together about things that suited the occasion.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Little did I think, back when my husband went on pilgrimage, that I would ever follow him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: And you thought just as little of lying in his bed, and resting in his room, as you are doing now.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: And still less did I ever think of seeing his face with comfort, and of worshiping the Lord the King with him; and yet now I believe I will.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Listen, don’t you hear a noise?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Yes, it is, I believe, the sound of music, for joy that we are here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Wonderful! Music in the house, music in the heart, and music also in heaven, for joy that we are here! They talked like this for a while, and then went to sleep.]

So in the morning when they were awake, Christiana said to Mercy, What made you laugh in your sleep last night? I suppose you were dreaming.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: So I was, and a sweet dream it was; but are you sure I laughed?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Yes, you laughed heartily; but please, Mercy, tell me your dream.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: I was dreaming that I sat all alone in a solitary place, bemoaning the hardness of my heart. Now I had not sat there long before, as I thought, many people gathered around me to look at me, and to hear what it was that I said. So they listened, and I went on bemoaning the hardness of my heart. At this, some of them laughed at me, some called me a fool, and some began to push me around. With that, I thought I looked up and saw someone with wings coming towards me. He came straight to me and said, Mercy, what is wrong? Now when he had heard me make my complaint, he said, Peace be to you; he also wiped my eyes with his handkerchief, and clothed me in silver and gold. Ezek 16:8–11. He put a chain around my neck, and earrings in my ears, and a beautiful crown on my head. Then he took me by the hand and said, Mercy, come after me. So he went up, and I followed until we came to a golden gate. Then he knocked; and when those inside had opened it, the man went in, and I followed him up to a throne, on which one sat; and he said to me, Welcome, daughter. The place looked bright and twinkling, like the stars, or rather like the sun, and I thought that I saw your husband there; so I awoke from my dream. But did I laugh?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Laugh! Yes, and well you might, to see yourself so well. For you must let me tell you that it was a good dream; and that, as you have begun to find the first part true, so you will find the second true at last. “God speaks in one way and in another, yet no one notices: in a dream, in a vision in the night, when deep sleep falls upon men as they slumber on their beds.” Job 33:14, 15. We do not need to lie awake in bed to talk with God; he can visit us while we sleep, and make us hear his voice then. Our heart often wakes when we sleep, and God can speak to it, by words, by proverbs, by signs and similitudes, as well as if one were awake.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Well, I am glad of my dream; for I hope before long to see it come true, and make me laugh again.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: I think it is now high time to get up, and to find out what we are to do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Please, if they invite us to stay a while, let us gladly accept the offer. I am all the more willing to stay here a while, to get better acquainted with these young women: I think Prudence, Piety, and Charity have very lovely and sober faces.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: We will see what they do.]

So when they were up and dressed, they came down, and asked one another how they had slept, and whether it had been comfortable or not.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Very good, said Mercy: it was one of the best nights’ lodging I have ever had in my life.]

Then Prudence and Piety said, If you can be persuaded to stay here a while, you shall have whatever the house can provide.

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: Yes, and very gladly, said Charity. So they agreed, and stayed there about a month or more, and did one another much good. And because Prudence wanted to see how Christiana had brought up her children, she asked her permission to catechize them. So she gave her consent freely. Then Prudence began with the youngest, whose name was James.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And she said, Come, James, can you tell me who made you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Good boy. And can you tell me who saved you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Good boy again. But how does God the Father save you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: By his grace.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: How does God the Son save you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: By his righteousness, death and blood, and life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And how does God the Holy Spirit save you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: By his illumination, by his renovation, and by his preservation.]

Then Prudence said to Christiana, You are to be commended for bringing up your children like this. I suppose I need not ask the others these questions, since the youngest of them can answer them so well. So I will now turn to the next youngest.

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Then she said, Come, Joseph (for his name was Joseph), will you let me catechize you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: With all my heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: A reasonable creature, made so by God, as my brother said.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What does this word, saved, imply?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: That man, by sin, has brought himself into a state of captivity and misery.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is implied by his being saved by the Trinity?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: That sin is so great and mighty a tyrant that no one can pull us out of its clutches but God; and that God is so good and loving to man as actually to pull him out of this miserable state.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is God’s purpose in saving poor men?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: The glorifying of his name, of his grace and justice, etc., and the everlasting happiness of his creature.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Who are those who will be saved?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: Those who accept his salvation.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Good boy, Joseph; your mother has taught you well, and you have listened to what she has told you.]

Then Prudence said to Samuel, who was the second eldest,

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Come, Samuel, are you willing for me to catechize you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: Yes, indeed, if you please.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: A place and state most blessed, because God dwells there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is hell?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: A place and state most wretched, because it is the dwelling-place of sin, the devil, and death.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Why do you want to go to heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: So that I may see God, and serve him without growing weary; that I may see Christ, and love him forever; that I may have that fullness of the Holy Spirit in me which I can by no means enjoy here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: A very good boy, and one who has learned well.]

Then she turned to the eldest, whose name was Matthew; and she said to him, Come, Matthew, shall I catechize you too?

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Very gladly.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: I ask, then: was there ever anything that existed prior to, or before, God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: No, for God is eternal; nor was there anything, except himself, that existed until the beginning of the first day. For in six days the Lord made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that is in them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What do you think of the Bible?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: It is the holy word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Is there anything written in it that you do not understand?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Yes, a great deal.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What do you do when you come across passages in it that you do not understand?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: I think God is wiser than I. I also pray that he will be pleased to let me know everything in it that he knows will be for my good.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What do you believe about the resurrection of the dead?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: I believe they will rise the very same that were buried; the same in nature, though not in corruption. And I believe this for two reasons: first, because God has promised it; secondly, because he is able to do it.]

Then Prudence said to the boys, You must keep listening to your mother; for she can teach you more. You must also listen carefully to whatever good talk you hear from others: for it is for your sakes that they speak good things. Notice also, and carefully, what the heavens and the earth teach you; but above all, meditate much on that book which caused your father to become a pilgrim. I, for my part, my children, will teach you what I can while you are here, and will be glad if you ask me questions that lead to godly edification.

Now when these pilgrims had been at this place for a week, Mercy had a visitor who professed some affection for her, and his name was Mr. Brisk; a man of some breeding, who professed religion, but a man who clung very close to the world. So he came once or twice, or more, to Mercy, and offered her his love. Now Mercy was fair of face, and so all the more attractive.

Her mind, too, was set on always keeping herself busy doing things; for when she had nothing to do for herself, she would be making stockings and clothes for others, and would give them to those who were in need. And Mr. Brisk, not knowing where or how she disposed of what she made, seemed to be greatly taken with her, because he never found her idle. I am sure she is a good housewife, he said to himself.

Mercy then told the young women of the house about the matter, and asked them about him, for they knew him better than she did. So they told her that he was a very busy young man, and one who professed religion, but was, they feared, a stranger to the power of what is good.

Well then, said Mercy, I will look at him no more; for I mean never to have a drag on my soul.

Prudence then answered that there was no need to discourage him much; if Mercy only kept on as she had begun, doing for the poor, that would soon cool his ardor.

So the next time he comes, he finds her at her old work, making things for the poor. Then he said, What, always at it? Yes, she said, either for myself or for others. And how much can you earn a day? he said. I do these things, she said, that I may be rich in good works, laying up in store for myself a good foundation for the time to come, that I may lay hold on eternal life. 1 Tim 6:17–19. Why, tell me, what do you do with them? he said. Clothe the naked, she said. With that his face fell. So he stopped coming to her. And when he was asked why, he said that Mercy was a pretty lass, but troubled with bad ways.

When he had left her, Prudence said, Did I not tell you that Mr. Brisk would soon abandon you? Yes, and he will spread a bad report about you; for, in spite of his profession of religion, and his seeming love for Mercy, Mercy and he are of dispositions so different that I believe they will never come together.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: I might have had husbands before now, though I did not speak of it to anyone; but they were men who did not like my ways, though none of them ever found fault with my looks. So they and I could not agree.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Mercy in our days is valued very little except in name: as for the practice of it, which your ways show, there are only a few who can bear it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Well, said Mercy, if nobody will have me, I will die unmarried, or my ways will be a husband to me: for I cannot change my nature; and to have a husband who crosses me in this is something I mean never to allow as long as I live. I had a sister named Bountiful, who was married to one of these misers, but he and she could never agree; and because my sister was resolved to go on as she had begun, that is, to show kindness to the poor, her husband first denounced her at the market cross, and then turned her out of his house.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And yet he was a professing Christian, no doubt?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Yes, such a one as he was, and the world is now full of men like him: but I will have none of them, not one.]

Now Matthew, the eldest son of Christiana, fell sick, and his sickness was severe, for he had great pains in his bowels, so that at times the pain doubled him up, pulling, as it were, both ends of him together. There also lived, not far from there, a Mr. Skill, an old and well-tried physician. Christiana asked for him, so they sent for him, and he came. When he had come into the room, and had watched the boy a little, he concluded that he was sick with colic. Then he said to his mother, What has Matthew’s diet been lately? Diet! said Christiana, nothing but what is wholesome. The physician answered, This boy has been meddling with something that lies undigested in his stomach, and that will not go away without treatment. And I tell you, he must be purged, or else he will die.

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: Then Samuel said, Mother, what was it that my brother picked up and ate as soon as we had come from the gate at the head of this way? You know that there was an orchard on the left, on the other side of the wall, and some of the trees hung over the wall, and my brother picked the fruit and ate it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: True, my child, said Christiana, he did take some, and ate it: naughty boy that he was, I scolded him, and still he would eat it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: I knew he had eaten something that was not wholesome food; and that food, that is, that fruit, is in fact the most harmful of all. It is the fruit of Beelzebub’s orchard. I am amazed that no one warned you of it; many have died of it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana began to cry, and she said, Oh, naughty boy! and oh, careless mother! What shall I do for my son?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: Come, do not be too downcast; the boy may get well again, but he must purge and vomit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Please, sir, use all your skill on him, whatever it costs.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: Well, I hope I will be reasonable. So he made him a purge, but it was too weak; it was said to be made of the blood of a goat, the ashes of a heifer, and some of the juice of hyssop. Heb 9:13, 19; 10:1–4. When Mr. Skill saw that that purge was too weak, he made one strong enough to do its work. It was made #emph[ex carne et sanguine Christi],#footnote[Of the flesh and blood of Christ.] John 6:54–57; Heb 9:14. (You know that physicians give their patients strange medicines.) And it was made into pills, with a promise or two, and a proportionate amount of salt. Mark 9:49. Now, he was to take them three at a time, on an empty stomach, in half a quarter of a pint of the tears of repentance. Zech 12:10.]

When this medicine was prepared, and brought to the boy, he was unwilling to take it, though he was so torn with colic that it seemed he would be pulled to pieces. Come, come, said the physician, you must take it. It turns my stomach, said the boy. I insist that you take it, said his mother. I will vomit it up again, said the boy. Please, sir, said Christiana to Mr. Skill, how does it taste? It has no bad taste, said the doctor; and with that she touched one of the pills with the tip of her tongue. Oh, Matthew, she said, this medicine is sweeter than honey. If you love your mother, if you love your brothers, if you love Mercy, if you love your life, take it. So after much fuss, and a short prayer for God’s blessing on it, he took it, and it worked well in him. It made him purge; it made him sleep, and rest quietly; it put him into a fine warmth and a light sweat, and rid him of his colic completely. So in a little while he got up, and walked about with a staff, and would go from room to room and talk with Prudence, Piety, and Charity about his illness, and how he was healed.

So when the boy was healed, Christiana asked Mr. Skill, Sir, what will repay you for your trouble and care over my child? And he said, You must pay the master of the College of Physicians, Heb 13:11–15, according to the rules laid down for such cases.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But, sir, she said, what else is this pill good for?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: It is a universal pill; it is good against all the diseases that pilgrims are prone to; and when it is well prepared, it will keep good indefinitely.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Please, sir, make me up twelve boxes of them; for if I can get these, I will never take any other medicine.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: These pills are good to prevent diseases, as well as to cure them when one is sick. Yes, I dare say it, and will stand by it, that if a man will only use this medicine as he should, it will make him live forever. John 6:51. But, good Christiana, you must give these pills in no other way than as I have prescribed; for if you do, they will do no good. So he gave Christiana medicine for herself, and her boys, and for Mercy; and told Matthew to beware of eating any more green plums; and kissed them, and went his way.]

You were told earlier that Prudence had told the boys that if at any time they wished, they should ask her questions that might do them good, and she would say something to them.

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Then Matthew, who had been sick, asked her why medicine is, for the most part, bitter to our taste.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show how unwelcome the word of God and its effects are to a carnal heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why does medicine, if it does good, purge, and make one vomit?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the word, when it works effectually, cleanses the heart and mind. For you see, what the one does to the body, the other does to the soul.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: What should we learn from seeing the flame of our fire go upwards, and from seeing the beams and sweet influences of the sun strike downwards?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: From the fire going up, we are taught to rise to heaven by fervent and burning desires. And from the sun sending its heat, beams, and sweet influences downwards, we are taught that the Savior of the world, though high, reaches down with his grace and love to us below.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Where do the clouds get their water?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Out of the sea.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: What may we learn from that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: That ministers should draw their doctrine from God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why do they empty themselves on the earth?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that ministers should give out to the world what they know of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why is the rainbow caused by the sun?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the covenant of God’s grace is confirmed to us in Christ.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why do the springs come from the sea to us through the earth?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the grace of God comes to us through the body of Christ.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why do some of the springs rise out of the tops of high hills?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the Spirit of grace will spring up in some who are great and mighty, as well as in many who are poor and low.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why does the fire take hold of the candle-wick?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that unless grace kindles in the heart, there will be no true light of life in us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why are the wick and tallow and all used up to keep the candle alight?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that body and soul, and all, should be at the service of that grace of God that is in us, and spend themselves to keep it in good condition.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why does the pelican pierce her own breast with her beak?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To nourish her young with her blood, and so to show that Christ the blessed so loved his young (his people) as to save them from death by his blood.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: What may one learn from hearing the cock crow?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Learn to remember Peter’s sin, and Peter’s repentance. The cock’s crowing also shows that day is coming: so let the crowing of the cock remind you of that last and terrible day of judgment.]

Now about this time their month was up; so they told those of the house that it was time for them to be up and going. Then Joseph said to his mother, You should not forget to send to the house of Mr. Interpreter, to ask him to let Mr. Great-Heart be sent to us, so that he may be our conductor for the rest of the way. Good boy, she said, I had almost forgotten. So she drew up a petition, and asked Mr. Watchful the porter to send it by some suitable man to her good friend Mr. Interpreter; and when it had come, and he had seen what the petition said, he said to the messenger, Go, tell them that I will send him.

When the household where Christiana was staying saw that they meant to go on, they called the whole house together to give thanks to their King for sending them such edifying guests as these. When that was done, they said to Christiana, And may we not show you something, as our custom is with pilgrims, for you to meditate on when you are on the way? So they took Christiana, her children, and Mercy into the private room, and showed them one of the apples that Eve ate, and that she also gave to her husband, and for the eating of which they were both turned out of paradise, and asked her what she thought it was. Then Christiana said, It is food or poison, I do not know which. So they explained the matter to her, and she held up her hands and wondered. Gen 3:6; Rom 7:24.

Then they took her to a place, and showed her Jacob’s ladder. Gen 28:12. Now at that time there were some angels ascending on it. So Christiana looked and looked to see the angels go up; so did the rest of the company. Then they were about to go into another place, to show them something else; but James said to his mother, Please, ask them to stay here a little longer, for this is a rare sight. So they turned back, and stood feasting their eyes on so pleasant a view.

After this, they took them into a place where a golden anchor hung. They told Christiana to take it down; for, they said, You shall have it with you, for it is absolutely necessary that you should, so that you may lay hold of that within the veil, Heb 6:19, and stand steadfast if you meet with stormy weather, Joel 3:16: so they were glad of it.

Then they took them to the mount on which Abraham our father offered up Isaac his son, and showed them the altar, the wood, the fire, and the knife, for they remain there to be seen to this very day. Gen 22:9. When they had seen it, they held up their hands, and marveled, and said, Oh, what a man Abraham was, for love to his Master and for denial of himself!

After they had shown them all these things, Prudence took them into a dining room, where there stood a pair of excellent virginals;#footnote[A musical instrument.] so she played on them, and turned what she had shown them into this excellent song, singing,

#quote[“Eve’s apple we have showed you; \
Of that be you aware: \
You have seen Jacob’s ladder too, \
Upon which angels are. \
An anchor you received have; \
But let not these suffice, \
Until with Abra’m you have gave \
Your best, a sacrifice.”]

Now, about this time, someone knocked at the door; so the Porter opened it, and there was Mr. Great-Heart. But when he had come in, what joy there was! For it now came fresh into their minds how only a little while ago he had killed old Grim Bloody-man the giant, and had delivered them from the lions.

Then Mr. Great-Heart said to Christiana and to Mercy, My Lord has sent each of you a bottle of wine, and also some parched corn, together with a couple of pomegranates; he has also sent the boys some figs and raisins, to refresh you on your way.

Then they set out on their journey, and Prudence and Piety went along with them. When they came to the gate, Christiana asked the Porter if anyone had gone by lately. He said, No; only one, some time ago, who also told me that there had lately been a great robbery committed on the King’s highway, the way you are going. But, he said, the thieves have been caught, and will soon be tried for their lives. Then Christiana and Mercy were afraid; but Matthew said, Mother, there is nothing to fear, as long as Mr. Great-Heart is to go with us, and to be our conductor.

Then Christiana said to the Porter, Sir, I am much obliged to you for all the kindnesses that you have shown me since I came here; and also because you have been so loving and kind to my children. I do not know how to repay your kindness; so please, as a token of my respect for you, accept this small mite. So she put a gold angel#footnote[A gold coin worth ten shillings.] in his hand; and he bowed low to her, and said, “Let your garments always be white, and let your head never lack oil.” Eccl 9:8. Let Mercy live and not die, and let not her works be few. Deut 33:6. And to the boys he said, Flee youthful lusts, and follow after godliness with those who are grave and wise, 2 Tim 2:22: so you will put gladness into your mother’s heart, and win praise from all who are sober-minded. So they thanked the Porter, and departed.
